    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d122-a1">

                <pb facs="0257" n="169" sameAs="frus:frus1892/pg169"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure in No. 77.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Thompson to Mr.
                            Coolidge.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1892-11-08"><hi rendition="#italic">November 8,
                        1892</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Sir</hi>: Referring to the ease of J. Maurice
                    Hubbard, correspondence concerning which is already on file in your office, may
                    I ask that you will again apply to the State Department for permission to issue
                    a passport to Mr. Hubbard. Unquestionably he is an American citizen by birth and
                    his long residence in France is due to his attendance upon his mother.</p>
                <p>The facts are before you, so I will not trouble you with any further detail of
                    them, except to add that, at his request, I have been trying, as his guardian,
                    to find a situation for him in the United States which would enable him to
                    continue his contribution toward the support of his mother, she being largely
                    dependent thereon.</p>
                <p>With great respect, I remain, yours, truly,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Robert M. Thompson</hi>,<lb/><hi
                            rendition="#italic">37 Wall Street, New York.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
